    "q-332913": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332913",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$ {{field-1}} $\\cdot \\,\\,$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot {e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -3[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b \\\\ 2a\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3a \\\\ -3b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2b+1 \\\\ 2a+5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2b+1=-3a \\\\ 2a+5=-3b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n-2b+1=-3a\\,⇒\\,a=\\frac{2b-1}{3}.\n$$\n\n \nSubstituting this into the second equation and solving for $b,$ we have\n\n$$\n2(\\frac{2b-1}{3})+5=-3b\\,⇒\\,4b-2+15=-9b\\,⇒\\,b=-1.\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na=\\frac{2(-1)-1}{3}=\\frac{-2-1}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
