    "q-329265": {
      "dom_id": "step-q329265",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0))\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2(0)}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2}=4 \\\\ 2{c}_{1}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{2}=4.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
